  "output": {
    "answer": "I write the gate first — the pass/fail result — before either lane runs. Then I spin up two isolated worktrees off the same base: `git worktree add ../lane-a -b lane-a/<slug>` (and the same for lane-b), so concurrent lanes never collide in one shared directory. Each lane gets its own brief and runs independently. A stronger model reviews the output one tier up, stating exactly what it checked. To merge back once the gate passes, I run `git merge --no-ff lane-a/<slug> -m \"merge lane-a: <what was learned>\"`, with the commit body recording what the losing lane got right or wrong.",
